message:
I am thinking...: OK, so `hb_hash` first tries priority<1> which is `hb_deref(v).hash()`. For a `const object_t *`, `hb_deref` would dereference the pointer and call `.hash()`. So it does call `object_t::hash()`.

And `object_t::hash()`:
```cpp
uint32_t hash () const
{
    return hb_bytes_t (head, tail - head).hash () ^
           links.as_bytes ().hash ();
}
```

This hashes the content bytes of the serialized object. If those bytes are uninitialized, MSAN would trigger.

Now, in the `bucket_for` function of `hb_hashmap_t`:
```cpp
unsigned int bucket_for (K key) const
{
    unsigned int i = hb_hash (key) % prime;
    unsigned int step = 0;
    unsigned int tombstone = (unsigned) -1;
    while (!items[i].is_unused ())
    {
        if (items[i] == key)
            return i;
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

The `items[i] == key` check uses `item_t::operator==` which compares `hb_deref(key) == hb_deref(o)`, which calls `object_t::operator==`:
```cpp
bool operator == (const object_t &o) const
{
    return (tail - head == o.tail - o.head)
        && (links.length == o.links.length)
        && 0 == hb_memcmp (head, o.head, tail - head)
        && links.as_bytes () == o.links.as_bytes ();
}
```

This also reads the content bytes. So either the hash or the comparison would trigger MSAN if content bytes are uninitialized.

Now the key question: where do uninitialized bytes come from in a serialized object?

